      "title": "Diving Into Data: Stories of Data-Inspired Journeys and Destinations",
      "summary": "What stories can data tell us\u2014and what can we learn from the process of uncovering them? In this month\u2019s episode of the Harvard Data Science Review Podcast, we speak with statistician Radu V. Craiu, the new editor of HDSR \u2019s Diving Into Data column, about the power of data to reveal surprising insights and the challenges of turning complex analyses into meaningful discoveries. Drawing on his research in statistical methodology and collaborations across fields such as genetics, astronomy, and demography, Craiu explores the importance of asking the right questions, recognizing the limitations of data, and learning from both successful analyses and those that don't go as planned. He also reflects on how artificial intelligence is reshaping the role of statisticians and why human judgment and statistical inference remain essential. The conversation also introduces Craiu\u2019s vision for Diving Into Data , a column dedicated to making the often-hidden stories behind data science more accessible. In his call for submissions , published in HDSR\u2019 s Fall 2026 issue (8.4), Craiu invites researchers and practitioners to share the discoveries, challenges, decisions, and even failures that shape their work with data, offering readers a glimpse into the process of data science. Our guest: Radu V. Craiu is a professor of statistical sciences at the University of Toronto and the editor of HDSR \u2019s Diving Into Data column. His research interests span computational methods in statistics, especially, Markov chain Monte Carlo algorithms (MCMC), Bayesian inference, copula models, model selection procedures, and statistical genetics.",
      "summary_html": "<p>What stories can data tell us\u2014and what can we learn from the process of uncovering them? In this month\u2019s episode of the <em>Harvard Data Science Review </em>Podcast, we speak with statistician Radu V. Craiu, the new editor of <em>HDSR</em>\u2019s Diving Into Data column, about the power of data to reveal surprising insights and the challenges of turning complex analyses into meaningful discoveries.</p>\n<p>Drawing on his research in statistical methodology and collaborations across fields such as genetics, astronomy, and demography, Craiu explores the importance of asking the right questions, recognizing the limitations of data, and learning from both successful analyses and those that don't go as planned. He also reflects on how artificial intelligence is reshaping the role of statisticians and why human judgment and statistical inference remain essential.</p>\n<p>The conversation also introduces Craiu\u2019s vision for <a href=\"https://hdsr.mitpress.mit.edu/diving-into-data\">Diving Into Data</a>, a column dedicated to making the often-hidden stories behind data science more accessible. In his <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1162/99608f92.40aced1c\">call for submissions</a>, published in <em>HDSR\u2019</em>s Fall 2026 issue (8.4), Craiu invites researchers and practitioners to share the discoveries, challenges, decisions, and even failures that shape their work with data, offering readers a glimpse into the process of data science.</p>\n<p>Our guest:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Radu V. Craiu is a professor of statistical sciences at the University of Toronto and the editor of <em>HDSR</em>\u2019s Diving Into Data column. His research interests span computational methods in statistics, especially, Markov chain Monte Carlo algorithms (MCMC), Bayesian inference, copula models, model selection procedures, and statistical genetics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>\u00a0</p>",
      "link": "https://HDSR.podbean.com/e/diving-into-data-stories-of-data-inspired-journeys-and-destinations/",
